INT. STATE PAROLE OFFICE - DAY

GLORIA (V.O.)
Eleven years running parolees. You learn that nobody ever comes in on time, and nobody ever leaves happy.

GLORIA PRUITT, 50s, reading glasses on a chain, sits at a desk stacked with folders. A wall clock TICKS. A fluorescent tube BUZZES.

HARLAN, 40s, orange tie, sits across from her holding a paper cup. Next to him, WENDELL, 40s, wears the same tie.

GLORIA
You two are brothers?

HARLAN
Twins.

WENDELL ^
Fraternal.

GLORIA (V.O.)
Both released the same morning. Both wearing the same tie. The state, in its wisdom, issued one tie per cell.

PETE, the clerk, sticks his head in the door.

PETE
Ms. Pruitt, your husband's on line two.

The phone on her desk BUZZES. She picks it up.

FRANK (V.O.)
(on the phone)
Did you eat? You didn't eat.

GLORIA
I'm working, Frank.

Harlan sips from the cup. It is empty. He keeps sipping anyway.
